---
date: 2026-08-18
topic: trailblazers
level: large
---

Pierre Janssen pointed a spectroscope at a total eclipse in India today in 1868 and found a yellow line that belonged to no element anyone knew about. Norman Lockyer caught the same line from London that October and named it for the sun — helium. Neither of them could produce a single atom of the stuff. It took twenty-seven years before anyone found it on Earth, and it turned out to be the second most common thing in the universe. For everyone who writes down the measurement they can't explain instead of throwing it out.